#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.746pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(519.6pt, 767.52pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 142.44pt, "Frequency upconversion techniques have demonstrated both high efficiency and excellent coherence in converting ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 155.28pt, "infrared photons to the visible spectrum [16,17]. Materials such as silicon carbide and quantum dots have emerged as ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 168.24pt, "promising candidates for bridging classical and quantum photonics, owing to their favorable optical properties and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 181.08pt, "compatibility with scalable device fabrication [17–20]. These advances in quantum transduction are propelling the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 193.92pt, "development of quantum communication networks, optical quantum computing platforms, telecom interconnects, and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 206.76pt, "distributed quantum architectures [21–24], bringing the vision of large-scale, interconnected quantum systems closer ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 219.6pt, "to reality. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 247.2pt, "Key insights from these studies can be summarized as follows: coherence preservation is often inferred indirectly ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 260.04pt, "through proxies such as conversion efficiency and added noise, with some approaches predicting near-unity efficiency ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 272.88pt, "under ideal conditions of intraband entanglement[25]. Direct measurements of input fidelity remain limited, reported ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 285.72pt, "explicitly in only a few cases, notably the acoustic bus system and teleportation-based protocols[26]. Scalability also ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 298.56pt, "varies significantly across platforms: electro-optic systems, while compact, encounter difficulties in maintaining high ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 311.52pt, "nonlinearity and quality factors at scale, whereas acoustic, phononic, and spin-based architectures offer greater ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 324.36pt, "potential for scaling to large qubit numbers and enabling reconfigurable quantum connectivity[27–29]. These studies ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 337.2pt, "highlight that no single method currently optimizes all desired metrics simultaneously, and performance comparisons ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 350.04pt, "are further complicated by the differing figures of merit employed across platforms. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 377.52pt, "Amid the various approaches to quantum transduction, including optical-to-microwave, optomechanical, and electro-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 390.48pt, "optic schemes, photonic quantum transduction, particularly in the form of direct optical-to-optical frequency ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 403.32pt, "conversion, has emerged as a promising and technically scalable solution as part of the quantum transduction ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 416.16pt, "ecosystem. All-photonic transduction enables coherent conversion between photons of vastly different energies while ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 429pt, "preserving essential quantum attributes such as phase coherence and entanglement. This capability is critical for ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 441.84pt, "bridging heterogeneous quantum systems, such as linking atomic quantum memories with fiber-based telecom ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 454.8pt, "networks[4,17,30–32]. Of particular relevance, this process enables the combination of atomic quantum memories ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 467.64pt, "with fiber optic communication channels[33]. In such networks, the optimal transmission wavelengths in fibers, which ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 480.48pt, "lie in the infrared spectrum of telecommunications wavelengths, may significantly differ from the wavelengths at ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 493.44pt, "which quantum information can be locally generated, stored, or processed—typically in the UV to blue spectrum ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 506.28pt, "range[34–36]. The substantial energy difference (~2.4 eV) between the UV and telecom wavelengths has constrained ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 519.24pt, "state-of-the-art quantum converters to quasi-phase matching in periodically poled lithium niobate (PPLN) waveguides ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 532.08pt, "[34,37]. Still, the performance and error tolerance suffer substantially for practical long-haul quantum network ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 544.92pt, "connectors due to their high susceptibility to photorefractive damage.  ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 569.4pt, "Alternative nonlinear processes are being explored to overcome these material and performance limitations, with ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 582.24pt, "stimulated emission such as four-wave mixing (FWM) transduction emerging as a particularly promising ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 595.2pt, "candidate[30,38–45]. FWM has demonstrated measurable advantages in both theoretical and experimental studies[46]. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 608.04pt, "For example, in a five-level atomic system, conversion efficiencies have reached approximately 33% under specific ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 620.88pt, "conditions and approach unity with increasing input power, accompanied by a reduction in the required conversion ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 633.72pt, "length[47]. Fiber- and intracavity-based models further reveal that FWM techniques can achieve lower noise levels ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 646.68pt, "and more accurate quantum state transfer compared to squeezed phase fluctuations, which in some cases lack explicit ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 659.64pt, "noise quantification or suffer from excess noise due to parametric amplification[48]. Furthermore, the spatiotemporal ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 672.48pt, "quasi-phase matching (QPM) in broadband fiber-based FWM has also enabled reconfigurable control of nonlinear ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 685.32pt, "optical devices through tunable spectral shaping with active pump modulation[49]. These findings, supported by ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 698.16pt, "theoretical analyses and numerical simulations across various platforms, underscore the potential of FWM-based ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 711pt, "transduction to deliver distinct efficiency and fidelity benefits under optimized operational regimes, positioning it as ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 723.96pt, "a highly attractive solution for scalable quantum information networks. Implementing FWM effectively, however, ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.746pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(519.6pt, 767.52pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 114.96pt, "requires a physical platform capable of sustaining high intensities, providing tunable dispersion, and maintaining ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 127.8pt, "excellent optical coherence. Gas-filled hollow-core capillary fibers (HCFs) are particularly well-suited for this ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 140.64pt, "role[50,51]. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 165.24pt, "The spectral phase of light pulses plays a fundamental role in quantum optics, as it directly governs the temporal and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 178.2pt, "spectral structure of a photonic wavepacket. In quantum information science, where information is encoded not only ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 191.04pt, "in photon number but also in the coherent superposition of frequency and time modes, the spectral phase becomes a ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 203.88pt, "critical parameter for accurately describing and manipulating quantum states of light. The ability to reconstruct the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 216.72pt, "complex spectral amplitude, including phase information, at the single-photon level provides key insight into the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 229.68pt, "coherence properties of quantum states[52–54]. Spectral phase correlations between photons can also induce distinct ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 242.52pt, "quantum interference patterns, which are essential for applications such as boson sampling, quantum metrology, and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 255.36pt, "quantum communication protocols[55,56]. Therefore, understanding and controlling spectral phase in frequency ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 268.2pt, "conversion processes is crucial for advancing high-performance and phase-coherent quantum transduction schemes. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 292.68pt, "Historically, gas-filled HCFs have been used for the development of high-performance light sources by enabling pulse ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 305.64pt, "compression[50,57,58]. In this work, we investigate spectral phase mapping in three distinct frequency conversion ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 318.48pt, "regimes: (1) input infrared (IR) to output ultraviolet (UV) frequencies within a gas-filled HCF system, (2) telecom-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 331.44pt, "band (1550 nm) to UV (308 nm), and (3) telecom to visible (516 nm). These regimes are pre-selected to address ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 344.28pt, "spectral mismatches among various quantum platforms as examples. For instance, UV frequency comb generation for ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 357.12pt, "precision spectroscopy and optical clock platforms, which play a foundational role in quantum metrology and next-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 370.08pt, "generation atomic clocks[59,60]. Trapped-ion qubits and Rydberg-atom systems often operate in the UV region[61–")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 382.92pt, "63], while nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers in diamond and certain rare-earth-ion-doped crystals exhibit optical ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 395.76pt, "transitions near 516 nm[64–66]. Meanwhile, telecom-band photons are ideal for long-distance fiber-based quantum ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 408.6pt, "communication due to their low-loss transmission in standard optical fibers[67]. Therefore, realizing coherent and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 421.44pt, "low-noise transduction between telecom and UV/visible domains is a critical step toward enabling heterogeneous ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 434.4pt, "quantum networks. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 461.88pt, "To benchmark the phase preservation across these regimes, we focus on degenerate FWM, as shown in the standard ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 474.72pt, "system schematic (Figure 1a). This simplified configuration involves only two states (Figure 1b), offering a simplified ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 487.56pt, "platform for analyzing phase evolution and optimizing nonlinear coupling dynamics. The gas-filled HCF environment ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 500.4pt, "allows precise control over key external parameters, such as gas pressure and temperature, to achieve phase matching ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 513.36pt, "and minimize dispersion, both of which are essential for maintaining high spectral phase coherence and thus high-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 526.2pt, "fidelity wavefunction phase transfer. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 553.68pt, "Figure 1c conceptually illustrates different orders of nonlinear optical processes and their associated Hamiltonians. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 566.52pt, "The FWM process studied here is governed by third-order nonlinearity (χ3), associated with a quartic Hamiltonian. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 579.36pt, "This facilitates strong parametric coupling between intermediary field, signal field, and idler fields, enabling efficient ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 592.32pt, "frequency conversion with high phase fidelity. ")
]

