  #line-at(70.199pt, 509.874pt, "Figure 3. Spectral phase mapping correlations under (a) input 1550 nm, intermediary 515 nm and output field 303nm and (b)  input ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 521.633pt, "1550 nm, intermediary 775 nm and output field 516 nm: between the output output field and output input pulses, and between the ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 533.154pt, "output output field and input input pulse, as a function of varying input intermediary energy and the FWHM of the intermediary ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 544.673pt, "pulse.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.694pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.199pt, 568.798pt, "To quantify the phase coherence and fidelity of the conversion process, we conducted a correlation analysis between ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 581.634pt, "the spectral phase of the input pulse and the generated output field pulses within their bandwidth. The correlation ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 594.595pt, "coefficient, which quantitatively evaluates the fidelity of phase transfer, spans from -1 to 1, with 1 representing perfect ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 607.437pt, "coherence and -1 indicating complete anti-correlation, reflecting a total loss of phase mapping. Figure 2d illustrates ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 620.279pt, "the correlation between the signal pulse and the output idler field pulse across varying intermediary energy and initial ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 633.115pt, "pulse bandwidths (FWHM). Even at relatively high intermediary energy and broader input bandwidths, the correlation ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 645.957pt, "remains above 0.95. This high correlation reflects not only classical waveform fidelity, but also implies that the ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 658.914pt, "underlying quantum wavefunction’s spectral-temporal coherence is maintained throughout the conversion process. ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 671.756pt, "The quantum mechanical analog of these manipulations suggests a deep connection between spectral-temporal degrees ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 684.592pt, "of freedom and intrinsic photonic quantum properties[72]. As the FWHM of the input pulse increases, the coherence ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 697.434pt, "of the phase mapping declines notably. This behavior arises because the transmitted spectral phase is predominantly ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 710.276pt, "governed by the bandwidth of the intermediary, which is determined by the convolution of the input pulse spectrum ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 723.236pt, "with the intermediary spectrum itself. Similarly, increasing the intermediary energy leads to a sharp reduction in phase ")
]

// arXiv source page 8
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.746pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(519.6pt, 767.52pt, "8 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 114.96pt, "mapping coherence. This degradation is attributed to the onset of nonlinear effects, such as self-phase modulation ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 127.8pt, "(SPM) and cross-phase modulation (XPM), which induce spectral broadening of the intermediary and subsequently ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 140.64pt, "disrupt the fidelity of phase transfer during the conversion process. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 166.44pt, "Additionally, Figure 2e presents the correlation between the input pulse and the output field. The high correlation ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 179.28pt, "values (>0.99) at moderate intermediary energy (<10 μJ) and bandwidths (<1.5 nm) further confirm the effective ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 192.12pt, "transfer of phase information from the input to the output field frequency domain. As expected, at extremely high ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 204.96pt, "pulse energies and broad bandwidths, the correlation slightly degrades due to the onset of nonlinear distortions. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 217.92pt, "Nevertheless, the overall high correlation values across a broad parameter space strongly support the preservation of ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 230.76pt, "spectral phase coherence through the parametric frequency mixing process. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 256.56pt, "Figure 3 presents the correlation analysis for two frequency conversion regimes: (a-b) 1550 nm to 308 nm and (c-d) ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 269.4pt, "1550 nm to 516 nm. In both cases, we examine the correlation between the output idler field and the output signal ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 282.36pt, "(left) as well as the input signal (right). In the 1550–308 nm conversion (Figure 3a), high correlation (>0.99) is ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 295.2pt, "maintained at low intermediary energy and narrow FWHM, but degrades sharply as these parameters increase. This ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 308.04pt, "decline reflects the growing impact of nonlinear effects, particularly SPM and XPM, which distort the intermediary ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 320.88pt, "spectrum and disrupt spectral phase coherence. The correlation with the output signal is slightly more resilient than ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 333.72pt, "with the input signal, indicating partial preservation of internal symmetry despite overall phase degradation. For the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 346.68pt, "1550–516 nm case, the correlation remains consistently high (>0.95) across a broader range, with only moderate ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 359.52pt, "degradation at large FWHMs or high intermediary powers. This robustness arises from more favorable phase-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 372.36pt, "matching conditions and reduced nonlinearity at longer wavelengths. Notably, both input-output field and output-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 385.2pt, "output field correlations track similarly, confirming stable and symmetric phase transfer under realistic operating ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 398.04pt, "conditions. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 422.64pt, "2.2 Negative Quadratic Phase Modulation")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 447pt, "As established in method section, when the initial phase modulation is an even-order function, the input and output ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 459.84pt, "fields acquire phases of the opposite sign. To further explore this behavior, we apply a negative quadratic phase ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 472.8pt, "modulation, corresponding to an up-chirped pulse, to the input and examine the resulting phase transfer characteristics. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 498.48pt, "Figure 4a–c shows an example of the spectral intensity and phase profiles of the input/output intermediary, signal, and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 511.32pt, "idler fields under this condition, corresponding to the IR-to-UV conversion regime (case 1). The input intermediary ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 524.28pt, "(Figure 4a) remains nearly transform-limited with minimal phase modulation. In contrast, the signal pulse carries a ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 537.24pt, "clear negative quadratic phase, leading to an up-chirped profile (Figure 4b). The idler field (Figure 4c) develops a ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 550.08pt, "spectral phase that is approximately the mirror image of the signal’s phase (positive quadratic phase), confirming the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 562.92pt, "expected opposite-sign phase relationship dictated by the parametric mixing process.  ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 588.6pt, "To quantify the fidelity of the phase mapping, we analyze the correlation between the spectral phases of the output ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 601.56pt, "waves. Figure 4d presents the correlation between the idler field and output signal phases as a function of the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 614.4pt, "intermediary energy and intermediary’s bandwidth in the IR-to-UV regime (case 1). Similarly, Figure 4e shows the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 627.24pt, "correlation between the idler field and the original input signal field. In both cases, the correlation coefficients exhibit ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 640.08pt, "significant degradation in regions of high intermediary energy and broad bandwidths, indicating sensitivity to ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 652.92pt, "nonlinear effects, inherent loss, and phase distortion under these conditions. At lower energy and moderate bandwidths, ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 665.88pt, "the correlation remains high, affirming effective phase transfer. However, as both parameters increase, the correlation ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 678.72pt, "declines sharply due to the onset of nonlinear phenomena and dispersion, which perturb the spectral phase structure ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 691.56pt, "during propagation. ")
]

