  #line-at(70.199pt, 515.274pt, "Consistent with our expectations, all three conversion regimes exhibit increasing conversion efficiency with higher ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 528.116pt, "intermediary energy and broader intermediary bandwidths. This trend reflects the enhanced nonlinear interaction ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 541.076pt, "strength and increased spectral overlap between the intermediary and input pulses under these conditions. In the IR-")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 553.918pt, "to-UV regime (case 1) at different intermediary energy level, the linear phase modulation case yields the highest ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 566.757pt, "conversion efficiency, reaching up to 28.0% (Figure 8a). Applying a negative quadratic phase reduces the efficiency ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 579.596pt, "to approximately 27.6% (Figure 8b), due to temporal broadening and reduced peak intensity. The positive quadratic ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 592.435pt, "phase case shows intermediate performance, achieving up to 8.4% efficiency (Figure 8c). ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 618.356pt, "For the telecom-to-UV conversion (case 2), the overall conversion efficiencies are lower due to the increased phase ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 631.198pt, "mismatch and broader spectral separation. At different intermediary energy level, the maximum efficiency reaches ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 644.034pt, "8.4% in the linear case, while negative and positive quadratic modulation reduce the values to 4.3% and 2.6%, ")
