  #line-at(70.199pt, 669.836pt, "moderate maximum efficiency of 10.8%, while the negative and positive quadratic cases reduce the performance to ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 682.675pt, "11.3% and 3.2%, respectively. ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 708.353pt, "However, this improvement in conversion efficiency comes with an important trade-off. As shown in our previous ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 721.195pt, "sections, higher intermediary energies and broader bandwidths — while beneficial for efficiency — lead to significant ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 734.155pt, "degradation in phase coherence. Specifically, in the positive quadratic modulation case, the strong nonlinear effects ")
]

// arXiv source page 16
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.746pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(513.72pt, 767.52pt, "16 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.695pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.08pt, 114.96pt, "(SPM and XPM) not only broaden the output field spectrum but also distort the phase profile, undermining the fidelity ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 127.8pt, "of quantum information transfer. This highlights a crucial balance between achieving high conversion efficiency and ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 140.64pt, "maintaining phase coherence, which is essential for applications in coherent quantum state transduction. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 166.44pt, "Interestingly, the linear modulation case (Figures 8, 9, and 10a) offers the most favorable balance, delivering relatively ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 179.28pt, "high conversion efficiency while maintaining superior phase fidelity across a broad operational window. However, ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 192.12pt, "the impact of quadratic phase modulation differs across regimes. In the telecom-to-UV case, the negative quadratic ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 204.96pt, "modulation (Figure 9b) results in the lowest efficiency (~4%), likely due to temporal broadening that reduces the peak ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 217.92pt, "intensity and phase mismatch between the interacting waves. The positive quadratic modulation consistently leads to ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 230.76pt, "the lowest efficiency (2.6% and 3.2%, respectively), suggesting that the forward chirp introduces more detrimental ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 243.72pt, "temporal misalignment in these higher-frequency-separation conversions. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 269.4pt, "While increasing intermediary power and bandwidth can enhance nonlinear interaction and energy transfer, it also ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 282.36pt, "amplifies unwanted phase distortions, particularly under quadratic modulation. Therefore, achieving optimal ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 295.2pt, "performance requires a tailored parameter design, balancing conversion efficiency and phase coherence based on the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 308.04pt, "priorities of the application, whether focused on energy throughput or on preserving quantum phase information during ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 320.88pt, "frequency conversion. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 345.36pt, "In addition to providing a comprehensive analysis of phase coherence and conversion efficiency, our study offers ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 358.2pt, "significant impacts for applications in quantum transduction. Although our present investigation is conducted in ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 371.04pt, "classical observation, the observed high-fidelity spectral phase mapping strongly indicates the potential for extending ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 384pt, "this approach to quantum states. Specifically, our findings demonstrate that the spectral phase, a critical parameter for ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 396.84pt, "preserving quantum coherence, can be reliably transferred across different frequency domains using a gas-filled HCF ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 409.8pt, "platform driven by FWM processes. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 435.48pt, "A particularly important insight from our work is the evidence suggesting that quantum information can be mapped ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 448.32pt, "effectively from the input field to the output frequency-converted field and that the high coherence between the newly ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 461.16pt, "generated output field and the input field can also be maintained. This broader applicability expands the potential for ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 474.12pt, "connecting quantum systems that operate in diverse spectral regions, such as linking telecommunications-band ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 486.96pt, "quantum memories with visible-to-UV light quantum processors or detectors. Traditional frequency conversion ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 499.8pt, "techniques often face severe challenges related to phase noise accumulation and limited efficiency[73–75]. In contrast, ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 512.64pt, "our results show that with appropriate control of the intermediary bandwidth and energy ratio, it is possible to achieve ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 525.48pt, "both high conversion efficiency and preserved phase fidelity, laying a practical foundation for coherent quantum ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 538.44pt, "frequency bridging. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 564.12pt, "Our comparison across linear, negative quadratic, and positive quadratic modulation schemes reveals critical trade-")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 576.96pt, "offs that must be considered in the design of future quantum transducers. For instance, while the quadratic modulation ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 589.92pt, "can deliver high conversion efficiency, it does so at the cost of significant phase distortion, especially at higher ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 602.88pt, "intermediary bandwidths and energies. This distortion arises from the compounded effects of SPM, XPM, and GVD, ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 615.72pt, "which reshape the output field’s phase profile and diminish coherence. Conversely, the linear modulation format ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 628.56pt, "emerges as a more balanced configuration, sustaining both strong phase fidelity and reasonable efficiency across a ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 641.4pt, "broad operational window. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 667.2pt, "Furthermore, our analysis of the correlation coefficients provides insights into the underlying dynamics of spectral ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 680.04pt, "phase mapping. We observe that increasing the intermediary bandwidth beyond ~2 nm triggers a transition in the ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 692.88pt, "output field’s phase structure, driven by nonlinear spectral broadening. In the positive quadratic case, this leads to a ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 705.72pt, "phase curvature inversion followed by restoration to a positive parabolic shape, accompanied by pulse compression. ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 718.56pt, "Such behavior, while challenging for maintaining perfect coherence, could be exploited in quantum transduction ")
  #line-at(70.08pt, 731.52pt, "protocols that intentionally harness nonlinear reshaping effects for tailored quantum state preparation[76–78]. ")
]

// arXiv source page 17
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-17.png", width: 428pt), 96pt, 527pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.745pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(513.723pt, 767.513pt, "17 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.694pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.199pt, 115.08pt, "Moreover, a deeper understanding of these nonlinear-induced phase transformations could inform the development of ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 127.917pt, "quantum error corrections[79–81], where anticipated phase distortions are mitigated or actively compensated. By ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 140.758pt, "integrating appropriate error correction protocols that account for systematic phase shifts and spectral broadening, it ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 153.718pt, "may be possible to enhance the robustness of quantum information transfer, even in regimes where nonlinear effects ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 166.559pt, "are non-negligible. This not only validates the versatility of the gas-filled HCF platform for coherent frequency ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 179.401pt, "conversion but also charts a clear pathway for optimizing performance in future quantum applications across different ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 192.237pt, "platforms. By understanding and managing the balance between nonlinear enhancement and phase preservation, this ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 205.079pt, "approach holds strong promise for enabling robust, low-noise quantum networks[82–84]. As future work, ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 218.039pt, "experimental efforts focused on direct quantum state measurements and entanglement preservation will be critical to ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 230.88pt, "fully unlock the potential demonstrated here.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.679pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.201pt, 257.16pt, "3.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.694pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(87.722pt, 257.159pt, "Discussion")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.694pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#0d0d0d"))
  #line-at(70.199pt, 274.199pt, "This study has demonstrated the potential for implementing quantum transduction through coherent spectral phase ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 287.04pt, "mapping in a gas-filled hollow-core fiber platform. By systematically exploring three typical frequency conversion ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 299.877pt, "regimes, IR-to-UV, telecom-to-UV, and telecom-to-visible, we have shown that it is possible to transfer spectral phase ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 312.837pt, "information with high fidelity from the signal field to the generated idler field. Our analysis confirms not only the ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 325.679pt, "preservation of spectral phase coherence during the conversion process but also strong phase correlations between the ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 338.637pt, "input and output fields, highlighting the capability of our approach to support coherent quantum information transfer ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 351.479pt, "across disparate spectral domains. These findings go beyond conventional demonstrations of frequency conversion, ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 364.315pt, "offering new insights into the physical mechanisms necessary for phase-preserving quantum transduction. The ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 377.275pt, "comparative evaluation across the three regimes further reveals the distinct roles of spectral separation, nonlinear ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 390.117pt, "dynamics, and modulation format in determining the trade-offs between conversion efficiency and phase fidelity. With ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 402.959pt, "further experimental validation, particularly focusing on quantum state preservation and entanglement mapping, this ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 415.795pt, "platform holds strong promise for enabling practical, low-noise, and spectrally versatile quantum interfaces. Our ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 428.637pt, "results lay foundational groundwork for integrating heterogeneous quantum systems and advancing the development ")
  #line-at(70.199pt, 441.597pt, "of coherent, scalable quantum networks. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.679pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
